<div class="fs-tablewrap"><table class="fs-table">
<caption class="sr-only">The religions compared: beginnings, founders, scriptures, followers and spread</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Faith</th><th scope="col">Began</th><th scope="col">Founder or early teachers</th><th scope="col">Scriptures</th><th scope="col">Followers today</th><th scope="col">Spread</th></tr></thead>
<tbody id="fs-live">
<tr class="fs-g"><th scope="rowgroup" colspan="6">Practised today <span class="n">35</span></th></tr><tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/hinduism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Hinduism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Hinduism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1500 BCE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">South &amp; Southeast Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">The Indian subcontinent: the Vedic religion of the north-west from about 1500 BCE, with older and local traditions</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: its teachings are traced to the seers of the Vedas and a long line of sages and teachers</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Vedas and Upanishads; the epics Mahabharata (with the Bhagavad Gita) and Ramayana; the Puranas<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/rigveda">Rigveda</a> · <a href="/work/upanishads">Upanishads</a> · <a href="/work/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a> · <a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> · <a href="/work/ramayana">Ramayana</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">About 1.2 billion (2020), most of them in India and Nepal</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=hinduism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Chinese%20folk%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Chinese folk religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1250 BCE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Central &amp; East Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">China, from the beginnings of Chinese civilisation: ancestor veneration from prehistory and divination by 1250–1150 BCE; as popular religion distinct from the Three Teachings, primarily from the Song dynasty (960–1279).</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it grew from local cults of gods, immortals and ancestors, bound to villages, lineages and temples rather than to a founder or a church.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">None of its own: it draws on a vast heritage of sacred books, from the Confucian classics and the I Ching to the Taoist Daodejing, the precious scrolls (baojuan) of the folk sects, morality books and works of mythology.<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/i-ching">I Ching</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">Yang and Hu (2012) counted 55.5% of China’s adults, 578 million people; the China Family Panel Studies’ 2012 survey found 81% of China’s population (1 billion) worshipping gods and ancestors in the traditional way; in Taiwan 42.7% of adults (16 million) are self-identified folk believers.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=chinese-folk-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/zoroastrianism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Zoroastrianism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Zoroastrianism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1200 BCE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Ancient Near East</span></th><td data-l="Began">Eastern Iran or Central Asia, in the second or early first millennium BCE (the date is disputed)</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">The prophet Zarathustra (Zoroaster)<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great lives:</span> <a href="/life/zoroaster">Zoroaster</a></span></td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Avesta, whose oldest part is the Gathas, the hymns of Zarathustra; later Pahlavi texts such as the Denkard and the Bundahishn<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/avesta">Avesta</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">Perhaps 100,000 to 200,000, mainly in India (the Parsis), Iran and the diaspora</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=zoroastrianism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/judaism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Judaism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Judaism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1000 BCE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Ancient Near East</span></th><td data-l="Began">The land of Israel (ancient Canaan), in the first millennium BCE</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Traced by tradition to Abraham and to Moses, through whom the Torah was given; shaped by the prophets and the rabbis<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great lives:</span> <a href="/life/abraham">Abraham</a> · <a href="/life/moses">Moses</a></span></td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Hebrew Bible (Tanakh): the Torah, the Prophets and the Writings; the Mishnah and the Talmud<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> · <a href="/work/talmud">Talmud</a> · <a href="/work/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">About 15 million, most of them in Israel and the United States</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=judaism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/buddhism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Buddhism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Buddhism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 500 BCE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">South &amp; Southeast Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">The Ganges plain of north-east India (Magadha and Kosala), 5th century BCE</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha (“awakened one”)<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great lives:</span> <a href="/life/the-buddha">The Buddha</a> · <a href="/life/ashoka">Ashoka</a></span></td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Pali Canon (Tipitaka); the Mahayana sutras; the Tibetan Kangyur and Tengyur<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/pali-canon">Pali Canon</a> · <a href="/work/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra</a> · <a href="/work/edicts-of-ashoka">Edicts of Ashoka</a> · <a href="/work/sanchi">Sanchi</a> · <a href="/work/ajanta-caves">Ajanta Caves</a> · <a href="/work/mogao-caves">Mogao Caves</a> · <a href="/work/bagan">Bagan</a> · <a href="/work/todai-ji">Tōdai-ji</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">Several hundred million: estimates range from about 320 to 500 million, depending on how practice in East Asia is counted</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=buddhism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/confucianism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Confucianism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Confucianism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 500 BCE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Central &amp; East Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">The state of Lu, in present-day Shandong, China, late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Confucius (Kongzi, 551–479 BCE), with the later teachers Mencius and Xunzi<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great lives:</span> <a href="/life/confucius">Confucius</a></span></td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Five Classics; the Four Books: the Analects, the Mencius, the Great Learning and the Doctrine of the Mean<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/analects">Analects</a> · <a href="/work/i-ching">I Ching</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">Few people claim it as their religion, but its values of family, learning and ritual are shared by hundreds of millions across East Asia</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=confucianism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/jainism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Jainism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Jainism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 500 BCE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">South &amp; Southeast Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">Magadha, north-east India, 6th or 5th century BCE</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Mahavira, the 24th Tirthankara, after Parshvanatha and twenty-two earlier teachers of the tradition<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great lives:</span> <a href="/life/mahavira">Mahavira</a></span></td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Agamas of the Svetambara canon; the Shatkhandagama and the works of Kundakunda for the Digambaras; the Tattvartha Sutra, accepted by both</td><td data-l="Followers today">About 4.5 million in India (2011 census), with communities in Britain, North America and East Africa</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=jainism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/maya-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Maya%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Maya religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 500 BCE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Americas</span></th><td data-l="Began">The Maya area of Mesoamerica, existing for more than two and a half millennia as a recognizably distinct phenomenon; before Christianity it was spread over many indigenous kingdoms with their own local traditions</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None named: it grew within the wider frame of Mesoamerican religion, and its priests, diviners and tellers of tales are among its main sources</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Dresden, Madrid and Paris codices and the Grolier Codex, inscriptions and the 'ceramic codex' of pottery scenes; the Popol Vuh, the Ritual of the Bacabs and the Chilam Balam books from the early colonial period; a large oral tradition</td><td data-l="Followers today">The extant Maya peoples of Guatemala, Belize, western Honduras and southern Mexico, where the religion coexists with Christianity and the Pan-Maya movement</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=maya-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/daoism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Daoism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Daoism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 400 BCE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Central &amp; East Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">China, as a philosophy in the Warring States period (4th–3rd centuries BCE) and as an organised religion from 142 CE</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Laozi, by tradition the author of the Daodejing; Zhuangzi; Zhang Daoling, the first Celestial Master<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great lives:</span> <a href="/life/laozi">Laozi</a></span></td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Daodejing and the Zhuangzi; the Daoist Canon (Daozang), printed in 1445 and 1607, with some 1,500 texts<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/tao-te-ching">Tao Te Ching</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">Hard to count: Daoist priests and monastics serve temples in China, Taiwan and Chinese communities abroad, and its practices reach many millions more</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=daoism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/tengrism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Tengrism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Tengrism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 350 BCE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Central &amp; East Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">The Eurasian steppes, from the folk religions of the Turkic and Mongolic peoples; Chinese chronicles first record Tengri as the sky god of the Xiongnu in the 4th century BCE.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it formed from the various folk religions of the steppe peoples.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">None written as scripture: it is known from historical texts such as the Orkhon inscriptions, the Irk Bitig (9th century), the Secret History of the Mongols (1240) and the Altan Tobchi (17th century), and from prayers handed down orally.</td><td data-l="Followers today">Over one million in Kazakhstan (2024) and about 50,000 in Kyrgyzstan (2014), where it is not officially recognised; a small share of the people of Mongolia; communities in Sakha, Buryatia, Tuva and other parts of Siberia.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=tengrism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/shinto"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Shinto.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Shinto</a><span class="fs-span">c. 300 BCE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Central &amp; East Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">Japan, with roots in prehistoric times; named and organised around the imperial court from the 7th and 8th centuries</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it grew from the rites of Japan’s communities and clans</td><td data-l="Scriptures">No single scripture; the Kojiki (712) and the Nihon Shoki (720) record its myths, and the Engishiki (927) its rites and prayers</td><td data-l="Followers today">Most Japanese take part in its festivals and rites at some 80,000 shrines, though few call Shinto their religion</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=shinto">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/samaritanism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Samaritanism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Samaritanism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 120 BCE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Ancient Near East</span></th><td data-l="Began">Samaria, around Mount Gerizim. Samaritans trace their separate history to a schism in the time of the High Priest Eli; scholars see a distinct tradition emerging in the Hasmonean and Roman era, with the temple on Gerizim built in the 5th century BCE.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: Samaritanism holds itself to be the faith the Israelites kept under Moses, whom it regards as the final prophet; after him the only authority is the successive Samaritan priesthood, from the line of Aaron.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Samaritan Pentateuch (the Torah), the only scripture; Samaritans also keep a variation of the Book of Joshua, which they do not regard as scripture, and chronicles, halakhic texts, midrash and liturgy.</td><td data-l="Followers today">Approximately 900 registered communal Samaritans in 2025.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=samaritanism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/bon"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Bon.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Bon</a><span class="fs-span">c. 100 BCE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Central &amp; East Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">Tibet: Bonpos hold that it was taught in Olmo Lungring or Tazig, west of Tibet, and came first to Zhangzhung, around Mount Kailash, then to Tibet; scholars distinguish an ancient Bon of the era before 618 CE from a classical Yungdrung Bon of the 10th and 11th centuries.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Bonpos revere the Buddha Tonpa Shenrab Miwo as its founder, though no available sources establish his historicity.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Bon Kanjur (the words of Tonpa Shenrab, probably assembled by 1450) and Tenjur (commentaries and treatises), largely terma, treasure texts revealed from the 10th century on; the Zibji (gzi brjid), the life of Tonpa Shenrab.</td><td data-l="Followers today">About 400,000 on the Tibetan Plateau (scholars’ estimate, 2019), chiefly in eastern Tibet, with communities in the Himalayan regions, India and Nepal and Bonpo centres in cities around the world.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=bon">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/christianity"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Christianity.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Christianity</a><span class="fs-span">c. 30 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Ancient Near East</span></th><td data-l="Began">Roman Judea and Galilee, about 30 CE</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Jesus of Nazareth, whom Christians call the Christ; spread by his apostles, among them Peter and Paul<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great lives:</span> <a href="/life/jesus">Jesus</a> · <a href="/life/mary-mother-of-jesus">Mary, mother of Jesus</a> · <a href="/life/paul-the-apostle">Paul the Apostle</a> · <a href="/life/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a></span></td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Bible: the Old Testament (the Hebrew Bible, with further books in some churches) and the New Testament<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> · <a href="/work/new-testament">New Testament</a> · <a href="/work/book-of-kells">Book of Kells</a> · <a href="/work/gutenberg-bible">Gutenberg Bible</a> · <a href="/work/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre-Dame de Paris</a> · <a href="/work/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica</a> · <a href="/work/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">About 2.3 billion (2020): the largest religion in the world, with the most followers now in sub-Saharan Africa</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=christianity">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/mandaeism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Mandaeism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Mandaeism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 100 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</span></th><td data-l="Began">According to the Haran Gawaita, Nasoraean disciples of John the Baptist left Jerusalem in the first century CE and settled in southern Mesopotamia; scholars dispute whether the origin lay in Israel or in Mesopotamia.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: Mandaeans hold Adam to be the founder of the religion and John the Baptist its greatest and final prophet, who renews and reforms an ancient faith; they do not regard John as its founder.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Ginza Rabba (Left and Right Ginza), the Qulasta prayerbook, the Draša d-Yahya or Book of John, the Haran Gawaita, the Diwan Abatur and the Book of the Zodiac, in Mandaic.</td><td data-l="Followers today">Between 60,000 and 100,000 worldwide; 60,000 to 70,000 in 2007, when about 5,000 remained in Iraq; around 10,000 in Australia.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=mandaeism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/gnosticism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Gnosticism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Gnosticism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 150 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Egypt &amp; North Africa</span></th><td data-l="Began">The Mediterranean world, with Alexandria central to its birth; the origins cannot be traced exactly and are disputed, and the systems were fully developed by the mid-2nd century</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: a collection of groups and teachers, among them Valentinus (c. 100 – c. 180), Basilides of Alexandria and Marcion, rather than one founder</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Nag Hammadi library of 1945, with fifty-two mostly Gnostic treatises in Coptic, including the Gospel of Thomas and the Apocryphon of John; the Mandaean Ginza Rabba; and the reports of Church Fathers such as Irenaeus</td><td data-l="Followers today">Mandaeans, thought to number 60,000 to 70,000 worldwide, in Iraq, Iran and diaspora communities, and small modern Gnostic churches</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=gnosticism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/islam"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Islam.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Islam</a><span class="fs-span">610 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</span></th><td data-l="Began">Mecca and Medina, in western Arabia, from 610</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">The prophet Muhammad (c. 570–632), the last of the prophets in Islamic belief<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great lives:</span> <a href="/life/muhammad">Muhammad</a> · <a href="/life/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a></span></td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Quran; the hadith, reports of the words and deeds of Muhammad<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/quran">Quran</a> · <a href="/work/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> · <a href="/work/suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque</a> · <a href="/work/great-mosque-of-djenne">Great Mosque of Djenné</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">About 2 billion (2020), the second-largest religion; the largest Muslim country is Indonesia</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=islam">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/hawaiian-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Hawaiian%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Hawaiian religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 800 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Oceania</span></th><td data-l="Began">The Hawaiian Islands, brought by Tahitians and other Pacific Islanders who landed there between 500 and 1300 CE; there is significant evidence of settlement no later than 800 CE, with immigration continuing to about 1300 CE, from the Marquesas and greater Polynesia.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it arose among Polynesian settlers and evolved over time and from place to place; tradition says the kapu system arrived with Pāʻao, a priest or chief from Tahiti, around 1200 CE.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">None written: the Kumulipo, an epic chant linking the aliʻi, or Hawaiian royalty, to the gods, and the prayers, chants and traditions of the kahuna and families, handed down orally.</td><td data-l="Followers today">No figure is given: traditional practices survive through family ʻaumākua, the veneration of iwi (bones) and the care of sacred places; some Hawaiians practise Christianised versions of old traditions, others the old faith as a co-religion.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=hawaiian-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/yoruba-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Yoruba%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Yoruba religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1000 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Sub-Saharan Africa</span></th><td data-l="Began">The Yoruba lands of present-day south-western Nigeria, Benin and Togo; the sacred city of Ile-Ife flourished from around 1000 CE</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None; tradition honours Oduduwa, the first king of Ife, and the sage Orunmila, the prophet of Ifa</td><td data-l="Scriptures">An oral literature: the verses of Ifa divination, gathered under 256 signs called odu, and the praise poems and songs of the orisha</td><td data-l="Followers today">A minority of Yoruba today, beside Christianity and Islam, and across the Americas in Santería, Candomblé and related traditions</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=yoruba-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/druze-faith"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Druze%20faith.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Druze faith</a><span class="fs-span">1017 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</span></th><td data-l="Began">Cairo, in Fatimid Egypt, where Hamza ibn Ali began in 1017 to preach a Muwaḥḥidūn (Unitarian) doctrine that developed from the Ismāʿīlī branch of Shiʿa Islam; Wadi al-Taym in Lebanon is generally considered the birthplace of the faith.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Hamza ibn Ali ibn Ahmad, an Ismaili mystic from Khorasan, considered its founder and the primary author of its manuscripts; the Fatimid caliph al-Hakim bi-Amr Allah is its central figure, and al-Muqtana Baha’uddin led it after al-Hakim’s disappearance.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Epistles of Wisdom (Rasāʾil al-Ḥikma), its foundational text, with the Quran; also the Rasa’il al-Hind (Epistles of India) and other treatises. Only initiates may read the holy books.</td><td data-l="Followers today">Between 800,000 and one million (no year given); in 1998 about 40–50% lived in Syria, 30–40% in Lebanon, 6–7% in Israel and 1–2% in Jordan. In 2021 about 60,000 lived in Venezuela and around 50,000 in the United States.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=druze-faith">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/yazidism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Yazidism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Yazidism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1100 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</span></th><td data-l="Began">Kurdistan, from a pre-Islamic Kurdish religious substratum with deep roots in ancient Iranian beliefs, reorganised in the 12th and 13th centuries by Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir and his successors at Lalish, in the Shekhan region northeast of Mosul.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir (born in the 1070s, died 1162), the central saint of the faith, regarded as its reformer, renewer or founder; Yazidis venerate him as an earthly incarnation of God. Sheikh Hasan consolidated its institutions in the 1230s and 1240s.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">Mainly oral: the qewls, sacred hymns in Kurmanji attributed chiefly to Şêx Fexredîn and other disciples of Sheikh Adi, with prayers and other poetry; also the mişûr manuscripts, the earliest of 1207–1208. The Black Book and Book of Revelation published in 1911 and 1913 are generally held to be forgeries.</td><td data-l="Followers today">No world total is given: in Iraq estimates range from 70,000 to 500,000; Germany has more than 230,000; Armenia 35,272 (2011 census); Georgia about 18,000 (2002 census), perhaps 6,000 today; Syria perhaps 12,000 to 15,000.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=yazidism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/korean-shamanism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Korean%20shamanism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Korean shamanism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1100 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Central &amp; East Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">Korea; the origins are unclear and the earliest historical references to mudang date to the 12th century, though Musok is often perceived as ancient.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: there is no central authority and no single founder; Korean origin myths name the first mudang as the eight daughters of Bupŭ-Whasang or as granddaughters of a princess, and legend makes Tan'gun the national ancestor and first mudang.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">None written: Musok has no doctrine and is orally transmitted; mudang inherit their chants (muga) and recite origin myths (ponp'uri) in rituals.</td><td data-l="Followers today">Over 200,000 mudang in South Korea in the early 21st century, members of professional organisations; a late 20th-century Korean Gallup survey found that 38 percent of South Korean adults had used a mudang; Religious Intelligence puts about 16 percent of North Koreans as practising &quot;traditional ethnic&quot; religion.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=korean-shamanism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/sikhism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Sikhism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Sikhism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1499 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">South &amp; Southeast Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">The Punjab, about 1500</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Guru Nanak (1469–1539), followed by nine Gurus to Guru Gobind Singh (d. 1708)<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great lives:</span> <a href="/life/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a></span></td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Guru Granth Sahib, compiled by Guru Arjan in 1604 and completed under Guru Gobind Singh<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a> · <a href="/work/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">About 25 to 30 million, most of them in the Punjab, with large communities in Britain, Canada and the United States</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=sikhism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/haitian-vodou"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Haitian%20Vodou.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Haitian Vodou</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1512 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Americas</span></th><td data-l="Began">Hispaniola, in the French colony of Saint-Domingue that became Haiti, between the 16th and 19th centuries, among enslaved West and Central Africans, especially Fon and Bakongo; it took the form of Vodou around the mid-18th century.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it arose from the blending of several West and Central African traditional religions with Roman Catholicism and Freemasonry, and has no central authority or single leader.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">None written: its teachings pass on orally and through ritual, song and the langaj of the rites, though many practitioners have used texts since these appeared in the mid-20th century.</td><td data-l="Followers today">Never counted, as Haiti has had no accurate census; in 1992 Leslie Desmangles put the Haitian practitioners at six million. Most Haitians practise both Vodou and Catholicism, and communities live in the United States, Cuba and elsewhere.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=haitian-vodou">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/mormonism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Mormonism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Mormonism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1820 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Americas</span></th><td data-l="Began">Western New York, in the 1820s and 1830s, during the religious excitement of the Second Great Awakening; Smith said he received a vision in the spring of 1820 and organised the Church of Christ in 1830.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Joseph Smith, whom his followers saw as a modern-day prophet; after his killing in 1844 a succession crisis split the movement, and the largest group followed Brigham Young.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Bible (the Authorized King James Version in the LDS Church), the Book of Mormon, the Doctrine and Covenants and the Pearl of Great Price; the Joseph Smith Translation of the Bible has varying acceptance among denominations.</td><td data-l="Followers today">The LDS Church claimed a membership of 16 million in 2020; no total is given for the whole movement, from which one source estimates over 400 denominations have sprung.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=mormonism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/candomble"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Candombl%C3%A9.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Candomblé</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1830 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Americas</span></th><td data-l="Began">Brazil, mainly the Bahia region, in the 19th century, among Afro-Brazilian communities formed under the Atlantic slave trade; by the founding myth the first terreiro, the Ilê Axé Iyá Nassô Oká, was founded in Salvador in 1830.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: the religion arose through the blending of Yoruba, Fon and Bantu religions with Roman Catholicism, and it has no central authority; each terreiro is autonomous under its own mãe or pai de santo.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">None: Candomblé has no central sacred text. Its knowledge, the fundamentos, is passed on in the terreiros, and divination draws on odu, mythological stories linked to the patterns of cowrie shells.</td><td data-l="Followers today">One census report indicated that around 1.3 percent of Brazil's population identified as followers, a figure likely to reflect only initiates; a 1997 census recorded 1,144 terreiros in Salvador.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=candomble">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/tenrikyo"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Tenrikyo.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Tenrikyo</a><span class="fs-span">1838 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Central &amp; East Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">Japan, in 1838, when in Tenrikyo tradition God was revealed through Nakayama Miki, who was chosen as the Shrine of God; its headquarters stand at the Nakayama Residence in Tenri City.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Nakayama Miki (born 1798, died 1887), known to followers as Oyasama and regarded by them as the Shrine of God; Izo Iburi, one of her earliest followers, later gave revelatory leadership as the Honseki.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">Three scriptures (sangenten): the Ofudesaki, a collection of 1,711 waka poems composed by Nakayama from 1869 to 1882; the Mikagura-uta, the songs of the Service; and the Osashizu, the record of Izo Iburi's oral revelations. Three supplemental texts (jungenten) are also used.</td><td data-l="Followers today">In 2002 over 2 million followers worldwide were estimated; in 2025 the Agency for Cultural Affairs counted 1,121,541 followers in Japan, down from a high of 2,298,420 in 1974.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=tenrikyo">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/bahai-faith"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Bah%C3%A1%27%C3%AD%20Faith.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Bahá'í Faith</a><span class="fs-span">1844 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</span></th><td data-l="Began">Qajar Iran: the Báb’s declaration in Shiraz in 1844 and Bahá’u’lláh’s declaration in Baghdad in 1863</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Bahá’u’lláh (1817–1892), heralded by the Báb (1819–1850)</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The writings of Bahá’u’lláh, among them the Kitáb-i-Aqdas and the Kitáb-i-Íqán, with those of the Báb and the interpretations of ʻAbdu’l-Bahá</td><td data-l="Followers today">Estimates range from about five to eight million, in almost every country of the world</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=bahai-faith">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/spiritism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Spiritism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Spiritism</a><span class="fs-span">1857 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Europe</span></th><td data-l="Began">France, in the mid-19th century, with the publication of The Spirits’ Book in 1857 by Hippolyte Léon Denizard Rivail, writing as Allan Kardec, after he observed table-turning and the spiritualist currents of Europe and the United States.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Allan Kardec (Hippolyte Léon Denizard Rivail), a French writer and educator, who coined the term spiritisme in 1857 and directed the Revue Spirite until his death in 1869.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Spiritist Codification, five basic works by Kardec published between 1857 and 1868: The Spirits’ Book, The Mediums’ Book, The Gospel According to Spiritism, Heaven and Hell, and The Genesis, with complementary works such as What is Spiritism?, the Spiritist Review and Posthumous Works.</td><td data-l="Followers today">About 13 million worldwide according to data from 2005, in 36 countries according to the International Spiritist Council; about 3.8 million in Brazil in the census of 2010, the country with the most followers.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=spiritism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/jehovahs-witnesses"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Jehovah%27s%20Witnesses.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Jehovah's Witnesses</a><span class="fs-span">1870 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Americas</span></th><td data-l="Began">The United States: a Bible study group formed by Charles Taze Russell and others in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, in 1870, which became the Bible Student movement; the name Jehovah's witnesses was adopted in 1931.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Charles Taze Russell, who co-founded Zion's Watch Tower Tract Society in 1881; Joseph Franklin Rutherford, who kept control of the Society after Russell's death and adopted the name in 1931.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Protestant canon of the Bible, held to be inspired and inerrant, read in their own New World Translation of the Holy Scriptures (full version 1961); doctrine is stated through Watch Tower Society publications such as The Watchtower and Awake!</td><td data-l="Followers today">About 9.2 million members worldwide reported in 2025, with about 9 million publishers in about 119,500 congregations; independent demographic studies suggest only about half of those who identify as Witnesses are counted as active by the faith itself.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=jehovahs-witnesses">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/santeria"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Santer%C3%ADa.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Santería</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1880 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Americas</span></th><td data-l="Began">Cuba, in the cities of the west of the island, in the late 19th century, from the traditional Yoruba religion brought by enslaved West Africans, Roman Catholicism and Spiritism.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it has no single founder. It formed among Afro-Cuban communities, and a trained priesthood in the first houses of worship gave new initiates a degree of standardisation.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">None: there is no key sacred text. Knowledge passes mostly by oral teaching and by taking part in rites; since at least the 20th century some initiates have kept notebooks (libretas), and the oricha are known through stories called patakís.</td><td data-l="Followers today">The number of initiates is estimated to be in the high hundreds of thousands. For the early 21st century two scholars estimated that about 8% of Cubans, between 800,000 and 900,000 people, were initiates. The American Religious Identification Survey of 2001 estimated about 22,000 practitioners in the United States.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=santeria">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/ahmadiyya"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Ahmadiyya.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Ahmadiyya</a><span class="fs-span">1889 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">South &amp; Southeast Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">British India, formally on 23 March 1889, when Mirza Ghulam Ahmad took the oath of allegiance from a number of his companions at a home in Ludhiana; the movement took the name Ahmadiyya in 1900.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Mirza Ghulam Ahmad (1835–1908) of Qadian, who said he had been divinely appointed as the promised Messiah and Mahdi; since his death the Community has been led by a succession of Caliphs.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Quran, with the sunnah and the ahadith, including the Kutub al-Sittah; the writings and recorded sayings of Mirza Ghulam Ahmad, and the collected record of his dreams, visions and revelations, hold a place in the Community's own literature.</td><td data-l="Followers today">The community gives a figure of &quot;tens of millions&quot;; independent sources estimate between 10 and 20 million worldwide, and other estimates run from 15 to 20 million. It was established in 209 countries and territories by 2016.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=ahmadiyya">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/native-american-church"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Native%20American%20Church.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Native American Church</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1890 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Americas</span></th><td data-l="Began">The Oklahoma Territory (1890–1907) in the late nineteenth century, after peyote was introduced to the southern Great Plains from Mexico.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it grew among Native American peoples of the southern Great Plains; figures named in its development include Chevato, Jim Aton, John Wilson, Jonathan Koshiway and Victor Griffin.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">None: ceremonies draw on prayer, peyote songs and drumming; the Cross-fire way also uses Bible readings, and the Half-Moon way uses no Bible.</td><td data-l="Followers today">An estimated 300,000 adherents in the United States, Canada and Mexico.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=native-american-church">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/caodaism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Caodaism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Caodaism</a><span class="fs-span">1925 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">South &amp; Southeast Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">Saigon, Southern Vietnam, in 1925, as a Spiritualist club; officially established in the city of Tây Ninh in 1926.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Cao Quỳnh Cư, Cao Hoài Sang, Lê Văn Trung and Phạm Công Tắc, clerks in the colonial government who founded the Spiritualist club in 1925; Ngô Văn Chiêu, revered as the first disciple of the Đức Cao Đài, signed the declaration of founding on 7 October 1926.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">Several sets of scriptures; those of the Tây Ninh Holy See include Kinh Thiên Đạo Và Thế Đạo (Prayers of the Heavenly and the Earthly Way), Pháp Chánh Truyền (the Religious Constitution), Tân Luật (the Canonical Codes) and Con Đường Thiêng Liêng Hằng Sống (Divine Path to Eternal Life).</td><td data-l="Followers today">Estimates vary: government figures give 4.4 million affiliated to the Tây Ninh Holy See and up to 6 million with other branches; the United Nations estimated about 2.5 million in Vietnam in January 2015; tens of thousands live abroad, mainly in North America, Cambodia, Europe and Australia.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=caodaism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="1"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/rastafari"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Rastafari.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Rastafari</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1930 CE – present</span><span class="fs-reg">Americas</span></th><td data-l="Began">Jamaica, in the 1930s, among impoverished and socially disenfranchised Afro-Jamaican communities, after clergymen proclaimed that Haile Selassie's 1930 crowning as Emperor of Ethiopia fulfilled biblical prophecy.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: there is no single founder and no leader followed by all Rastafari. Early preachers who proclaimed Haile Selassie the returned Jesus included Leonard Howell, Archibald Dunkley, Robert Hinds and Joseph Hibbert, and Howell was the early movement's leading figure.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Bible, often read literally and as cryptographic, with the Book of Revelation seen as especially important; also Leonard Howell's The Promised Key (1935), Robert Athlyi Rogers' Holy Piby (1924), Fitz Balintine Pettersburg's Royal Parchment Scroll of Black Supremacy (1920s) and, for many Rastas, the 14th-century Ethiopian Kebra Nagast.</td><td data-l="Followers today">An estimated 700,000 to 1,000,000 worldwide in 2012; 29,026 people identified as Rastas in Jamaica's 2011 census, and about 5,000 in England and Wales in the 2001 census.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=rastafari">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
</tbody>
<tbody id="fs-ended">
<tr class="fs-g"><th scope="rowgroup" colspan="6">No longer practised <span class="n">16</span></th></tr><tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/mesopotamian-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Mesopotamian%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Mesopotamian religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 3500 BCE – c. 500 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">Ancient Near East</span></th><td data-l="Began">Ancient Mesopotamia, above all Sumer in the south; its undercurrents are believed to go back to the first half of the 6th millennium BCE, and its first clear evidence comes with the invention of writing around 3500 BCE</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: a tradition governed by usage rather than by any official decision, which grew over millennia among Sumerian and Akkadian speakers</td><td data-l="Scriptures">No single scripture: myths, prayers, incantations and lists of gods written in cuneiform on clay tablets, in Sumerian, Akkadian or Aramaic, among them the Enuma Elish and the Epic of Gilgamesh<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">None: it had almost completely died out by about 400–500 CE, after its indigenous adherents had largely become Syriac Christians</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=mesopotamian-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Ancient%20Egyptian%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Ancient Egyptian religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 3100 BCE – c. 537 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">Egypt &amp; North Africa</span></th><td data-l="Began">The Nile valley, with prehistoric roots; shaped with the first kings around 3100 BCE</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it grew from the cults of Egypt’s towns and kings; the pharaoh Akhenaten briefly imposed the worship of the Aten in the 14th century BCE</td><td data-l="Scriptures">No single scripture: the Pyramid Texts, Coffin Texts and Book of the Dead for the afterlife, with hymns, myths and temple inscriptions<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/book-of-the-dead">Book of the Dead</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">None as a living tradition: the last temple, of Isis at Philae, closed around 537; small modern revivals draw on it</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=ancient-egyptian-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/canaanite-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Canaanite%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Canaanite religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 3000 BCE – c. 400 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">Ancient Near East</span></th><td data-l="Began">The land of Canaan in the southern Levant, during approximately the first three millennia BCE; first attested in the Ebla tablets of the second half of the 3rd millennium BCE</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: a tradition of many peoples and cities, strongly influenced by its Egyptian and Mesopotamian neighbours</td><td data-l="Scriptures">No scripture: myths, hymns, prayers and ritual texts on clay tablets, above all the Ugaritic texts of about 1275 BCE, with later inscriptions, the accounts of the Hebrew Bible and Greek writers such as Philo of Byblos</td><td data-l="Followers today">None: its last branch, the Punic religion of the western Mediterranean, survived in some places until the 4th century CE</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=canaanite-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/hittite-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Hittite%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Hittite religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1600 BCE – c. 1180 BCE</span><span class="fs-reg">Ancient Near East</span></th><td data-l="Began">Anatolia, in the Hittite empire centred there from c. 1600 to 1180 BCE, with its capital at Hattusa; a mix of Hattian, Hurrian and Hittite influences</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: a religion of a kingdom, drawing on Hattian, Hurrian and Mesopotamian traditions, with no single founder named</td><td data-l="Scriptures">No canonical scriptures: the surviving tablets are cult inventories, temple and ritual administration, diviners' reports, rituals and fragments of myth, some used in training scribes</td><td data-l="Followers today">None: the religion ended with the Hittite empire c. 1180 BCE, when the Hittite sites were burned</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=hittite-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/vedic-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Vedic%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Vedic religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1500 BCE – c. 500 BCE</span><span class="fs-reg">South &amp; Southeast Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">The northwest Indian subcontinent (Punjab and the western Ganges plain), in the early Vedic period from c. 1500 BCE, with roots in the Indo-Iranian religion of the Sintashta and Andronovo cultures</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it grew out of Indo-Iranian religion and the Bactria–Margiana culture, then took shape in the northwest of the subcontinent</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The Vedas, the four Samhitas with the Brahmanas, Aranyakas and some of the older Upanishads, composed in Vedic Sanskrit and first handed down by oral transmission<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/rigveda">Rigveda</a> · <a href="/work/upanishads">Upanishads</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">None as a religion of its own after the Vedic period, which is held to have ended around 500 BCE; it became Brahmanism and then Hinduism, and some Vedic rituals are still practised today</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=vedic-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Ancient%20Greek%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Ancient Greek religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1400 BCE – c. 500 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">Europe</span></th><td data-l="Began">The Aegean world: gods such as Zeus, Hera, Poseidon and Dionysus are named on Mycenaean tablets of the 14th and 13th centuries BCE</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None; its myths took their classic form in the poems of Homer and Hesiod around 700 BCE</td><td data-l="Scriptures">No sacred book: the epics of Homer, Hesiod’s Theogony, the Homeric Hymns and the sacred laws and calendars of each city<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/iliad">Iliad</a> · <a href="/work/odyssey">Odyssey</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">None as an ancient tradition: the Christian emperors banned its sacrifices in the 390s; a modern revival was recognised in Greece in 2017</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=ancient-greek-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Mesoamerican%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Mesoamerican religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1200 BCE – 1697 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">Americas</span></th><td data-l="Began">Mesoamerica, from central Mexico to Honduras, with the Olmec civilisation from about 1200 BCE</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it was shared and transformed over three thousand years by Olmecs, Zapotecs, Maya, Teotihuacanos, Toltecs, Mixtecs and Aztecs</td><td data-l="Scriptures">Painted screenfold books, of which only a handful survive, such as the Dresden Codex and the Codex Borgia; the Popol Vuh of the K’iche' Maya, written down in the 16th century<span class="fs-more"><span class="fs-more-l">Great works:</span> <a href="/work/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh</a></span></td><td data-l="Followers today">None as a public religion since the Spanish conquest; Maya day-keepers still count the 260-day calendar, and many old traditions live on within Catholic practice</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=mesoamerican-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/etruscan-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Etruscan%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Etruscan religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 900 BCE – c. 100 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">Europe</span></th><td data-l="Began">Etruria, in the Villanovan culture; the earliest evidence for religious practices dates to the Villanovan period, including a 9th century BCE shrine at Tarquinia</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: the Etruscans believed their religion had been revealed to them by seers, chiefly Tages, a childlike figure born from tilled land, and the female figure Vegoia</td><td data-l="Scriptures">None survive but the Liber Linteus, of about 800 surviving words; the Etrusca Disciplina, a corpus of sacred books, is known only from Roman writers</td><td data-l="Followers today">None: as the Etruscan civilisation was assimilated into the Roman Republic from the 4th century BCE its religion was absorbed into Roman culture, and knowledge of its language and religion soon ceased under the Julio-Claudians</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=etruscan-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/celtic-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Celtic%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Celtic religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 550 BCE – c. 600 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">Europe</span></th><td data-l="Began">Among the Celtic peoples of Iron Age Europe, as a descendant of Proto-Celtic paganism, itself derived from Proto-Indo-European religion; its earliest monumental sculptures, from Germany, date to the 6th and 5th centuries BCE.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it grew from older Indo-European religion, and many of its gods have cognates in other Indo-European mythologies.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">None written: the Celts wrote nothing down about their religion; it is known from archaeology, Greek and Roman writers, and Irish and Welsh tales written down by Christian monks in the Middle Ages.</td><td data-l="Followers today">None: it died out as the Celtic peoples converted to Christianity, in Gaul from the 3rd century and in Britain and Ireland from the 5th; Celtic neopaganism, a 20th-century new religious movement, draws on it.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=celtic-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/roman-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Roman%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Roman religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 509 BCE – 391 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">Europe</span></th><td data-l="Began">The city of Rome in Italy, shaped by Latin, Etruscan and Greek traditions; Roman legend traced most of its institutions to the city’s founders and kings, above all Numa Pompilius</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: legend credited Aeneas, Romulus and above all Numa Pompilius, the Sabine second king of Rome, with its rites, calendar and priesthoods</td><td data-l="Scriptures">No scripture: it rested on the correct practice of prayer, rite and sacrifice rather than on doctrine; the Sibylline books were consulted in crises, and calendars, priestly law and writers such as Cicero, Varro, Livy and Ovid preserve its lore</td><td data-l="Followers today">None: Nicene Christianity became the state religion in 380, and Theodosius I ended official support for all other cults in 391; modern groups such as the Associazione Tradizionale Pietas reconstruct its rites</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=roman-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/ajivika"><img src="/logos/thumbs/%C4%80j%C4%ABvika.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Ājīvika</a><span class="fs-span">c. 500 BCE – c. 1346 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">South &amp; Southeast Asia</span></th><td data-l="Began">Northern India, generally held to be in the 5th century BCE, though the exact origins are unknown; Buddhist and Jain texts name Savatthi (Śrāvastī) as the hub of the Ājīvikas</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">Makkhali Gosala (Sanskrit Gośala Maskariputra, c. 484 BCE) is generally considered the founder; some sources call him only the leader of a large congregation, and Jarl Charpentier and others suggest the tradition is older</td><td data-l="Scriptures">Lost: the primary texts are said to have included the ten Purvas (eight Mahanimittas and two Maggas) and the Onpatu Katir, but the movement is known only from the writings of Buddhists, Jains and others</td><td data-l="Followers today">None: the last dated inscription that mentions a tax on Ājīvikas is of 1346 CE, and stray references to survivors run to the 15th century</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=ajivika">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/mithraism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Mithraism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Mithraism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 80 CE – c. 400 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">Europe</span></th><td data-l="Began">The Roman Empire, where its earliest temples appear in the last quarter of the 1st century CE, apparently with its centre in Rome; inspired by Iranian worship of Mithra, though how far it continued Persian practice is debated</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None known: Merkelbach, Clauss and Beck think a founder or founders created the mysteries in Rome, while others see a continuation of Iranian Mithra worship</td><td data-l="Scriptures">None survive: no written narratives or theology, only an oath, a catechism fragment, graffiti and inscriptions, and passing references in Greek and Latin writers; the histories of Mithras by Pallas and Euboulus are lost</td><td data-l="Followers today">None: persecuted by Christians in the 4th century, it was suppressed and came to an end between the last decade of that century and the 5th century</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=mithraism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/manichaeism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Manichaeism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Manichaeism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 240 CE – c. 1600 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">Ancient Near East</span></th><td data-l="Began">Sasanian Mesopotamia, about 240</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">The prophet Mani (216–about 276), who claimed to complete the teachings of Zoroaster, the Buddha and Jesus</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The seven books of Mani, his Shabuhragan and his Picture Book, known today mainly from fragments found in Egypt and Central Asia</td><td data-l="Followers today">None today: the last communities, in southern China, faded after the 16th century</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=manichaeism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/norse-religion"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Norse%20religion.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Norse religion</a><span class="fs-span">c. 500 CE – c. 1100 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">Europe</span></th><td data-l="Began">Scandinavia, from the religion of the Germanic peoples of the Iron Age; best known from the Viking Age, about 800 to 1100</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it was led by chieftains, kings and seeresses, without a founder or a priestly order</td><td data-l="Scriptures">No scripture: its myths survive in skaldic poems, the Poetic Edda and Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda, written down by Christians in 13th-century Iceland</td><td data-l="Followers today">None as an ancient tradition: Scandinavia was Christian by the 12th century; a modern revival, Ásatrú, was recognised in Iceland in 1973</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=norse-religion">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/slavic-paganism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Slavic%20paganism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Slavic paganism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 500 CE – c. 1331 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">Europe</span></th><td data-l="Began">Among the Early Slavs of central, eastern and southeastern Europe (5th to 10th centuries CE), rooted in Proto-Indo-European religion; first described by the Byzantine historian Procopius of Caesarea in the 6th century.</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it grew from older Indo-European beliefs; in 980 Vladimir the Great tried to unify its cults in Kievan Rus’.</td><td data-l="Scriptures">None written: no original mythological texts survive; it is known from Byzantine, Arab and German writers, the Rus’ chronicles and sermons against paganism, archaeology and later folklore.</td><td data-l="Followers today">None: it gave way to Christianity between the 8th and 13th centuries, and a last known shrine, at Kobarid, was stamped out in 1331; its gods and rites lived on in folk religion, and the modern Rodnovery movement draws on it.</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=slavic-paganism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
<tr data-live="0"><th scope="row"><a class="fs-name" href="/religion/catharism"><img src="/logos/thumbs/Catharism.png" alt="" width="44" height="44" loading="lazy">Catharism</a><span class="fs-span">c. 1143 CE – c. 1350 CE</span><span class="fs-reg">Europe</span></th><td data-l="Began">The Rhineland, northern France, the Languedoc and northern Italy in the 12th century, first reported at Cologne in 1143; whether it came from the Byzantine Empire or arose locally is debated</td><td data-l="Founder or early teachers">None: it was taught at first by ascetic leaders who set few guidelines, and Catholic writers named it from outside</td><td data-l="Scriptures">The New Testament, the Bogomil Gospel of the Secret Supper, a modified Ascension of Isaiah and The Book of the Two Principles; few Cathar texts survive, as most were destroyed</td><td data-l="Followers today">None: the Inquisition, which claimed to have eradicated Catharism by 1350, ended it; the Languedoc's last known Perfect was executed in 1321</td><td data-l="Spread"><a class="fs-map" href="/faiths?faith=catharism">Map of its spread&nbsp;&rarr;</a></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p class="fs-note">Dates of beginning are those each profile gives, many of them approximate or traditional. Followers are counted as the profile's sources count them. <a href="/faiths">Faith Maps</a> shows the spread of each faith on a globe; <a href="/profiles?kind=life">Great Lives</a> and <a href="/profiles?kind=work">Great Works</a> hold the founders and the texts.</p>
